{"conjecture": "Round-trip equivalence testing is insufficient if it only validates semantic preservation from code back to specification; instead, the LLM must generate code together with a complementary executable witness (e.g., invariants or postconditions), and the repair loop must be driven by counterexamples that break this witness rather than the original spec. This forces the LLM to externalize its implicit reasoning into machine-checkable artifacts, making the translation verifiably faithful rather than merely plausible. The resulting technique—co-generation of code and witnesses with counterexample-guided joint repair—will produce specifications that hold their commitments at significantly higher rates than round-trip testing alone."}
